1. External lint engines — separate programs, invoked as subprocesses
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.0 Process isolation — why this section is separate

LintCrux does not embed, link, or statically or dynamically bind ANY lint
engine. Every engine adapter under `lib/services/engines/` shells out
through the `ProcessRunner` seam (`lib/services/engines/process_runner.dart`,
whose production implementation `SystemProcessRunner` calls
`dart:io`'s `Process.start`), spawns the engine as an independent
operating-system process, and parses the text or JSON the engine writes to
stdout/stderr. There is no `dart:ffi` usage anywhere in `lib/`, no
`DynamicLibrary` load, and no engine source, header, or object file in this
repository. The desktop CMake/Xcode build configurations under `linux/`,
`macos/`, and `windows/` contain only the stock Flutter runner targets.

That boundary matters legally as well as architecturally. Verilator is
LGPL/Artistic and GHDL is GPL; invoking them as separate programs across a
process boundary, exchanging only command-line arguments and diagnostic
text, keeps LintCrux's own Apache-2.0-licensed code cleanly separated from
their copyleft terms. A change that links an engine into the LintCrux
process would invalidate that analysis.

1.1 Distribution posture

As shipped today, LintCrux **does not distribute any engine binary**. The
engines are resolved at run time, in this order (see
`lib/services/engines/bundled_binary_resolver.dart`):

  1. the `LINTCRUX_BUNDLED_BIN_DIR` environment variable (used by CI and
     the integration-test harness),
  2. a Flutter bundled-asset path — declared but not yet resolvable; the
     resolver returns null here today,
  3. the host `PATH`.

In practice that means path (3): the user installs the engines. The version
pins in `tool/bundled_engines.yaml` and the
`.github/workflows/bundled-engines.yml` workflow are preparatory
infrastructure for a future bundled-binary release; that workflow's own
header states it "does not bundle binaries into the release build of the
Flutter app."

**When bundling activates, each engine's own license obligations attach to
the distribution** — including, for the GPL and LGPL engines, the
obligation to convey or offer the corresponding source of the binary being
distributed. That is a distribution question, not a licensing question
about LintCrux's own code, and it must be settled before any release ships
an engine binary. See §9.

